---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import { loadAllSnapshots, loadClassSnapshot, loadManifest } from '@data/loadSnapshot';
import { classChangeStats, formatDate, treeTalents } from '@data/derive';

const manifest = loadManifest();
const { ruleset } = manifest;
const druid = loadClassSnapshot('druid');
const byId = new Map(druid.talents.map((t) => [t.talentId, t]));
const stats = classChangeStats(druid);

const prereqCount = (s: { talents: { prerequisites: unknown[] }[] }) =>
  s.talents.reduce((n, t) => n + t.prerequisites.length, 0);
const druidPrereqs = prereqCount(druid);
const otherPrereqs = loadAllSnapshots()
  .filter((s) => s.classDef.classId !== 'druid')
  .map(prereqCount);
const otherMin = Math.min(...otherPrereqs);
const otherMax = Math.max(...otherPrereqs);

const trees = druid.trees.map((tree) => {
  const talents = treeTalents(druid, tree);
  const rows = Array.from({ length: tree.grid.rows }, (_, i) => ({
    row: i + 1,
    gate: ruleset.pointsPerRowTier * i,
    talents: talents.filter((t) => t.row === i),
  }));
  return {
    tree,
    talents,
    rows,
    totalRanks: talents.reduce((n, t) => n + t.maxRank, 0),
    singles: rows.filter((r) => r.talents.length === 1).map((r) => r.row),
    active: talents.filter((t) => !t.passive),
    capstones: rows[rows.length - 1].talents,
  };
});

const prerequisites = trees.flatMap(({ tree, talents }) =>
  talents.flatMap((t) =>
    t.prerequisites.map((p) => ({
      treeName: tree.name,
      talent: t.name,
      row: t.row + 1,
      needs: byId.get(p.talentId)?.name ?? p.talentId,
      rank: p.requiredRank,
    })),
  ),
);

const removed = druid.trees.map((tree) => ({
  tree: tree.name,
  names: tree.removedClassic.map((r) => r.name),
}));
const removedTotal = removed.reduce((n, r) => n + r.names.length, 0);
const joinList = (names: string[]) =>
  names.length <= 2
    ? names.join(' and ')
    : `${names.slice(0, -1).join(', ')}, and ${names[names.length - 1]}`;
---

<BlogPostLayout slug="wow-forever-druid-talent-trees-beta-data">
  <p>
    Most talent articles open with a verdict on which build is best. This one
    deliberately does not. The beta data does not contain damage numbers
    that would justify a ranking, and a list of talents with an opinion
    attached is easy to write and hard to trust. What the data does contain
    is structure: which talents sit in which row, how many ranks each has,
    what each one requires, and how it compares with the Classic version. For
    Druid that structure is worth reading on its own, because it determines
    what a {ruleset.maxBudget}-point build can physically look like.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything below is generated from the current snapshot (published
    {formatDate(manifest.publishedAt)}{manifest.gameBuild ? `, beta build ${manifest.gameBuild}` : ''}),
    so it will stay in step with the site's data. For rank-by-rank effect
    text, the
    <a href="/talents/druid/">Druid reference page</a> is the place to read,
    and the <a href="/druid/">Druid calculator</a> is the place to try
    things out.
  </p>

  <h2>The shape of the three trees</h2>
  <p>
    Druid has {druid.talents.length} talents across {trees.length} trees. Each
    tree is a grid of {trees[0].tree.grid.rows} rows. Row gates apply as they
    do for every class: the first row is open, and each later row needs
    {' '}{ruleset.pointsPerRowTier} more points in the rows above it, so the
    last row needs {ruleset.pointsPerRowTier * (trees[0].tree.grid.rows - 1)}.
    Our <a href="/blog/wow-forever-talent-points-levels-and-row-gates/">points and row gates explainer</a>
    covers that rule in detail.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Druid trees at a glance</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Tree</th>
          <th scope="col">Talents</th>
          <th scope="col">Total ranks</th>
          <th scope="col">Rows with a single talent</th>
          <th scope="col">Active abilities</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          trees.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{t.tree.name}</td>
              <td>{t.talents.length}</td>
              <td>{t.totalRanks}</td>
              <td>{t.singles.length ? t.singles.join(', ') : 'none'}</td>
              <td>{t.active.length}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A row with a single talent is a fixed point: there is no choice inside
    the row, and everything below it is gated by the points above. Active
    abilities are talents that are not passive, meaning they have a cost,
    cooldown or cast of their own in the data.
  </p>

  {
    trees.map((t) => (
      <>
        <h2>{t.tree.name}</h2>
        <p>
          {t.talents.length} talents and {t.totalRanks} total ranks.
          {t.active.length > 0 ? (
            <>
              {' '}The active abilities in this tree are{' '}
              {joinList(t.active.map((a) => a.name))}.
            </>
          ) : null}
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="data-table">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">{t.tree.name} talents by row</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Row</th>
                <th scope="col">Needs earlier</th>
                <th scope="col">Talents (max rank)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {t.rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.row}</td>
                  <td>{r.gate}</td>
                  <td>
                    {r.talents.length
                      ? r.talents.map((x) => `${x.name} (${x.maxRank})`).join(', ')
                      : 'none'}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond row gates, {druidPrereqs} Druid talents need a specific rank of
    another talent first. Other classes have between {otherMin} and
    {' '}{otherMax} such links in the current snapshot, so Druid is
    {druidPrereqs > otherMax ? ' the most connected class' : ' within the usual range'}
    on this measure. Each one below is a hard requirement: the calculator will
    not let you add the point until the prerequisite is met.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      prerequisites.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{p.talent}</strong> ({p.treeName}, row {p.row}) requires
          {' '}{p.needs} at rank {p.rank}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    The practical effect is that some talents come as a pair. If you want the
    dependent talent, you are also buying the prerequisite, and its point cost
    counts against the same budget. When you compare two builds, those pairs
    are easy to overlook, which is a good reason to test your plan in the
    calculator rather than count points in your head.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom row</h2>
  <p>
    The seventh row of each tree holds {joinList(
      trees.flatMap((t) => t.capstones.map((c) => `${c.name} (${t.tree.name})`)),
    )}. Reaching any of them means spending
    {' '}{ruleset.pointsPerRowTier * (trees[0].tree.grid.rows - 1)} points
    in that tree first, plus the point on the talent itself, which leaves
    {' '}{ruleset.maxBudget - ruleset.pointsPerRowTier * (trees[0].tree.grid.rows - 1) - 1}
    points of a full level-{ruleset.maxLevel} budget for everything else.
    Two bottom-row talents from different trees would need
    {' '}{2 * (ruleset.pointsPerRowTier * (trees[0].tree.grid.rows - 1) + 1)}
    points, which is {2 * (ruleset.pointsPerRowTier * (trees[0].tree.grid.rows - 1) + 1) > ruleset.maxBudget ? 'more than' : 'within'}
    the {ruleset.maxBudget}-point budget, since each one needs its own
    tree's gate points before it opens.
  </p>

  <h2>Compared with Classic</h2>
  <p>
    The data records a Classic reference for each talent. For Druid the
    split is {stats.new} new, {stats.changed} changed, {stats.moved} moved
    and {stats.unchanged} unchanged talents, and {removedTotal} Classic
    talents with no counterpart in the current trees:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      removed.map((r) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{r.tree}:</strong>
          {' '}{r.names.length ? joinList(r.names) : 'none removed'}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    "Changed" means the talent exists in both but something differs, such as
    its effect text or rank count. Each talent's reference entry spells out
    the specific difference, so a name you recognize from Classic is not a
    guarantee that it works the way you remember. The
    <a href="/changes/">Changes page</a> shows the same breakdown for every
    class.
  </p>

  <h2>Using this without a tier list</h2>
  <p>
    A few questions the structure can answer honestly: how early in the
    leveling process a given talent can exist, how many points a pair of
    linked talents costs together, and what is left over after committing to
    a bottom-row talent. Questions it cannot answer include which talent
    does the most damage or healing, or which tree is strongest. For that
    you need testing in game, and that testing may be out of date within a
    build or two.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rank texts here are labeled by how well we can vouch for them, which the
    <a href="/blog/where-wow-forever-talent-data-comes-from/">post on the data's origin</a>
    explains. If you spot a talent in the game that does not match what the
    reference page says for Druid, send a screenshot and the build number via
    the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
